<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=domestication-of-the-horse">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domestication of the horse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The domestication of the horse turned wild horses into domestic animals; genomic evidence places the source of all modern domestic horses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>–Don region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a>, in present-day southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, around 2200 BCE. Earlier claims, notably for the Botai culture of Kazakhstan in 3700–3500 BCE, remain debated. From this core area the domestic lineage spread rapidly across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> for transport, farm work and warfare, replacing other local horse populations. Horses buried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> in Sintashta and Petrovka graves of about 2100–1700 BCE give the most persuasive archaeological evidence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domestication-of-the-horse.jpg" alt="Domestication of the horse" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cave_painting_in_Doushe_cave,_Lorstan,_Iran,_8th_millennium_BC.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Horses appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> cave art as early as 30,000 BCE, at Lascaux among other places, but these were wild animals, probably hunted for meat. When domestication began depends partly on its definition. Some zoologists require human control over breeding, seen in ancient bones as smaller average size and greater variation; others weigh signs of work on skeletons and teeth, horse gear, art and burial customs. Horses were probably kept for meat and milk before they were trained to work. Genetic dates give only the latest possible moment, since wild and tame herds could interbreed for an unknown time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The oldest possible sign of a changed relationship comes from graves of the Khvalynsk and Samara cultures on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, about 4800–4400 BCE, where horse bones lie with those of sacrificed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep. At Botai settlements, with 50 to 150 pit houses, 65 to 99 percent of the discarded bones were horses, and pottery residues point to mare's milk; layers of dung and a corral at Krasnyi Yar suggest the animals were penned. Taylor and Barrón-Ortiz argued in 2021 that Botai shows intensive use of wild horses rather than true domestication.</p><p>Bones from Bell Beaker sites in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> of about 2500 BCE show the size changes of penned herds. Studies of DNA found 125 genes linked to domestication, and scientists suggest that genes for stronger backs (GSDMC) and docility (ZFPM1) made the spreading lineage suited to riding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the Sintashta and Petrovka chariot graves, horses were buried with pairs of antler cheekpieces whose inner studs pressed on the horse's lips when the reins were pulled, the oldest objects clearly identified as horse tack. Riding may have been practised in the 4th and 3rd millennia BCE, but the clearest effect of horses on ancient warfare came through the chariot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described the Sigynnae, who bred horses too small to ride but good at drawing chariots. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> mounted archers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> made cavalry a weapon of war, and over time the chariot fell out of use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Domestication of the horse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2200 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>The Volga–Don steppe, Russia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Earlier claims, at Botai in Kazakhstan (3700–3500 BCE) and on the steppe in the 5th millennium BCE, are debated.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/domestication">Domestication of animals<span>by 13,000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariot<span>c. 1950 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road and relay posts<span>c. 500 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stirrup">Stirrup<span>302 CE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/horseshoe">Horseshoe<span>414 CE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/horse-collar">Horse collar<span>477–499 CE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=domestication-of-the-horse">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
